Blocked on a double-thunk defect in the do-block lowering, not on anything
this test asserts.

`@try` over a body that is a call to a pure do-block function lowers to a
thunk whose value is ANOTHER thunk:

    def helper2():
        n0 = 5
        def helper1():
            ...
            return(n10)
        return(helper1)     # <-- the function, not its value
    n4 = morloc.mlc_try(helper2, ...)

so the Ok arm wraps a function object and serialization fails with
"Expected int for MORLOC_INT, but got function".

This is the same family as the historical @catch pure-do-block double wrap:
`thunkifyForTry` guards a body that is ALREADY a DoBlockN, but here the
inner thunk only appears further down, during the body's own lowering.
Collapsing a DoBlockN in tail position (including through lets) in Reduce.hs
does not reach it, so the nesting is not a plain DoBlockN-in-DoBlockN and
wants a proper look at where the inner thunk is minted.

The test itself -- optional widening under an effect -- is unrelated and
should be restored once the lowering is fixed.
